{"custom_id":"s-ap_calculus_bc-m07-k0","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgCxFyBpqHRuAcjVxFmCN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One petal of r = 3 sin(2θ) is traced as θ goes from 0 to π/2.\n\nArea = (1/2) ∫₀^{π/2} 9 sin²(2θ) dθ\n= (9/2) · (π/4)\n= 9π/8\n\nThis is choice index 1."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bboo4r93bFc1ozXxBZJxUd","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"1","brief_reasoning":"One petal is traced for θ from 0 to π/2. Area = (1/2)∫₀^{π/2} 9sin²(2θ)dθ = (9/2)(π/4) = 9π/8.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false,"choice_index":1,"final_answer":"9π/8"}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":260,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":267,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":260,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":267,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"cost":0.0066072}
{"custom_id":"s-ap_calculus_bc-m04-k1","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgCxFnoEscNnTiN4Rspry","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The answer is **3** (choice index 1).\n\n1. Find the derivatives:\n   - dx/dt = cos t\n   - dy/dt = −2 sin 2t = −4 sin t cos t\n\n2. A horizontal tangent needs dy/dt = 0 and dx/dt ≠ 0.\n\n3. Case sin t = 0, which gives t = 0, π and 2π. At each of these, cos t ≠ 0, so the tangent is horizontal.\n\n4. Case cos t = 0, which gives t = π/2 and 3π/2. Here both derivatives are zero, so take the limit: dy/dx = −4 sin t, which approaches −4 and +4. These slopes are not zero, so the tangents are not horizontal. You can check this another way: the curve is y = 1 − 2x², and at x = ±1 its slope is ∓4.\n\nThat leaves three values of t: 0, π and 2π. The question counts values of t, not points. So t = 0 and t = 2π both count, even though they land on the same point (0, 1)."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bs2HQwwxng89muUR4Rnzha","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"1","choice_index":1,"final_answer":"3","brief_reasoning":"dy/dt=-4sin t cos t, dx/dt=cos t. sin t=0 gives t=0,π,2π with dx/dt≠0. At cos t=0, dy/dx→-4sin t=±4, not horizontal. So there are 3 t-values.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":238,"cache_creation_input_tokens":568,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":568},"output_tokens":740,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":214},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":238,"cache_creation_input_tokens":568,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":568},"output